 This thesis sits at the intersection of two strands of work. One line studies early prediction of cascade outcomes and shows that forecasting eventual reach from partial trajectories is possible but difficult \citep{cheng_can_2014,szabo_predicting_2010}. A second line documents systematic diffusion differences between true and false information, largely using fully observed cascades \citep{vosoughi_spread_2018}. What remains less clear is how early these differences become actionable under a realistic early-warning constraint---that is, when models are restricted to the first $T$ minutes (e.g., 30/60/180 minutes) or the first $k$ messages of a cascade. This thesis connects these strands by testing whether diffusion-based signals provide predictive value within such early windows, and whether the timing of informativeness differs between reach prediction and veracity discrimination.
 
